---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import ProjectGrid from '../../components/ProjectGrid.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/projects';

export function getStaticPaths() {
	return projects.map((project) => ({
		params: { slug: project.slug },
		props: { project },
	}));
}

const { project } = Astro.props;

/* `images` is one shape now — { src, w, h, caption? } — so there is nothing to
   flatten here any more (2026-08-17; the bare-string form and the map that
   normalised it went together). See the field's note in projects.ts for why a
   caption and a size both ride on their own src rather than in parallel
   arrays, and why the string form had to go. */
const figures = project.images;

/* The caption's type is the prose's, exactly (2026-08-14, Hiro's ask): the same
   text-sm / leading-relaxed the two paragraphs above carry, with no colour and
   no weight of its own, so it inherits --ink and --weight-body the same way they
   do. That is the whole rule — if the prose moves, these follow, and this const
   should never grow a size or colour the paragraphs don't have.

   ⚠ It briefly ran at 0.7rem on --ink-soft, the about page's figcaption. That
   is the obvious treatment and it was overruled deliberately, so don't restore
   it as a fix: these are not descriptive captions, they are the labels the
   before/after argument turns on, and a label set quieter than the prose it is
   evidence for reads as an aside. The one thing worth keeping from that pass is
   what it was fixing — UNSTYLED, a figcaption inherits the 19px root and comes
   out BIGGER than the 16.625px prose. text-sm is what stops that; empty is not.

   Margin is BOTTOM because the caption sits above its image (see the template).
   0.625rem is 11.875px against the section's space-y-5 of 23.75px, so a label
   is always twice as close to the image it names as to the figure before it.
   That 2:1 is doing all the grouping work now that the type carries no contrast
   of its own — keep the ratio if the outer gap ever moves. */
const captionClass = 'mb-2.5 text-sm leading-relaxed';

/* ── THREE CARDS, THE THREE THAT FOLLOW THIS ONE (2026-08-16, Hiro: the strip
   should only show three projects) ──
   Not the first three of the list with the current one removed, and that
   distinction is the entire reason this is six lines instead of a .slice(0, 3)
   on the filter. Taking the top of a fixed list means the projects at the
   BOTTOM of projects.ts are never in it: at five projects, plain
   filter().slice(0, 3) shows migaki/ejs/stacksmith almost everywhere and
   renders Shipyard on exactly zero pages — the last entry can only be reached
   from the home grid, and the strip is the page's only way onward. A rule that
   makes one project unreachable is not a shortening of the list, it is a
   quiet unpublishing of the work at the end of it.

   Wrapping fixes that arithmetically rather than by taste: every project is
   the successor of exactly three others, so every project appears in exactly
   three strips, and clicking the FIRST card each time walks you through all
   five in projects.ts order and returns you to where you started. That is a
   real reading path, which is more than a "related work" list of any three.

   THE ORDER IS STILL projects.ts, ROTATED — never re-sorted. Same order the
   home grid uses, so the strip reads as that grid resumed from where you are
   rather than as a second, differently-sorted list. NOT sorted by year or
   "relatedness": the tags field hasn't been rendered anywhere since 07-29, and
   any similarity metric over a handful of items is theatre. The one visible
   cost is the wrap point — on the last project the strip runs migaki, ejs,
   stacksmith, so the row reads out of list order once per cycle. That is the
   price of every project being reachable and it is worth it.

   ⚠ NOT RANDOM, and don't make it so to "keep it fresh": this is a static
   build, so a random pick is frozen at build time anyway — it would just be an
   arbitrary fixed answer with no reason attached, and it would silently
   reintroduce the unreachable-project bug on some builds and not others.

   The strip asks ProjectGrid for three columns (2026-08-11, Hiro). That
   coupling is HAND-MAINTAINED, not derived: `columns` is a literal 3 down in
   the strip, not others.length. Deriving it was the obvious move and is the
   wrong one — the grid stops at three columns because a fourth shrinks the
   cards past their titles (ProjectGrid.astro has the measurements). The two
   threes now agree by construction, which is new: from 08-11 to today they did
   not, and four cards rendered three across with ONE ALONE ON ROW TWO. That
   ragged second row is what this change removes, and the note it replaces had
   chosen it deliberately over exactly this fix, on the grounds that capping
   the strip trades a ragged row for hiding work. The wrap is what settles that
   argument — nothing is hidden if everything is three clicks from everything.

   BELOW FIVE PROJECTS this simply yields fewer than three: the slice takes
   what there is, and at four projects it is still a full row. At two the
   strip disappears entirely — see the guard on the section below. */
const current = projects.findIndex((other) => other.slug === project.slug);
const others = [
	...projects.slice(current + 1),
	...projects.slice(0, current),
].slice(0, 3);
---

<Layout title={`${project.title} — Hiro Eernisse`} description={project.blurb}>
	<Nav />

	{/* ── sm:pb-16 (2026-08-11, Hiro) ──
	    All three routes end on the same footer element, so they should end on
	    the same amount of air, and this one was a step tighter than the other
	    two: the footer's own py-10 (47.5px) sits on top of whatever the page
	    leaves below its last block, and that was py-12's 57px here against 76px
	    on both home (#projects pb-16) and about (main sm:py-16) — 104.5px to
	    their 123.5px.

	    BOTTOM ONLY, deliberately asymmetric, which is why this is sm:pb-16 and
	    not about's symmetric sm:py-16: the top padding sets where the h1 lands
	    on arrival, and that is a settled decision this has no business moving.
	    The complaint was the footer.

	    ⚠ BELOW sm the three still disagree, and that is pre-existing rather than
	    something this introduced: home's pb-16 is unconditional (123.5px at
	    every width) while about's step is sm:py-16, so about and this page both
	    sit at 104.5px on a phone. Matching there means changing the home page or
	    about, not this file — left alone because the ask was this page's gap. */}
	<main class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-12 sm:pb-16">
		{/* No back link up here on purpose. It used to be that the sticky nav's
		    Projects link never left the viewport, so a second one this close read
		    as two of the same control; that link is gone (08-01), and the reason
		    is now simply that the footer's does the job better — it returns you
		    to the card you came from, at the scroll position you left it. */}

		{/* Dateline is the year alone — the company already reads off the prose
		    below, and tags/Visit were dropped from this page (both still live in
		    projects.ts).

		    ── 2026-08-10: THIS PAGE TAKES THE ABOUT PAGE'S SCALE AND PALETTE ──
		    Hiro's ask, and it applies to the whole page, so read this note before
		    changing any size or colour below. The project page had been running
		    its own type scale (title 35.625/42.75px, lead 23.75, body 21.375) and
		    its own two accents (--ink-title on the h1, --ink-num on the year).
		    The about page runs THREE values and no accents: headings at
		    --ink-heading, prose at 16.625px in plain --ink, ancillary text at
		    --ink-soft. That set is now this page's set too.

		    --ink-soft on the year, not --ink-num. The about page has no numerals,
		    so there was no counterpart to match — what it does have is small
		    ancillary text (the photo caption, the footer) and that runs
		    --ink-soft. The year rode ON the muted line already and the orange was
		    there to lift it out of it, which is the thing that goes.
		    ⚠ This was --ink-num's LAST use on the site; the card datelines dropped
		    it on 08-02 (see ProjectCard.astro). The token is still defined in
		    global.css and nothing consumes it. Left there rather than deleted —
		    that is a palette decision, not a page one.

		    NO sm:text-4xl. The h1 sat one step larger from sm up; it is one size
		    at every width now, 28.5px on a phone and on a desktop alike.

		    ── 2026-08-11: text-2xl, NOT the about page's text-3xl ──
		    Hiro's ask, "make the heading smaller", one step down the scale
		    (35.625 → 28.5px). This is the first place the two pages part company
		    since yesterday's pass above, and the note stays because that pass is
		    what a later reader will find first: the SCALE ANCHOR is no longer
		    "whatever the about page's greeting is". Colour, weight and tracking
		    are still that page's exactly; only this one number is ours.

		    It still clears the strip's h2 (text-xl / 23.75px) by a step, which is
		    what the page needs it to do — the h1 is the only text above the fold
		    and the h2 is several thousand pixels below it, so the pair never
		    shares a screen, but the two are the page's only headings and reading
		    them as one scale rather than two is the point. text-xl here would
		    have flattened that and is the step to avoid if "smaller" comes up
		    again; the honest next move at that point is dropping the h2 too. */}
		<header class="reveal" style="--i: 0">
			<p class="text-sm tabular-nums text-[var(--ink-soft)]">{project.year}</p>
			<h1 class="mt-2 text-2xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">{project.title}</h1>
		</header>

		{/* Reading matter is capped at --measure (see global.css) — ~11 words a
		    line — while the page itself spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill.

		    No "Background" / "Work" headings: two paragraphs in one screen give
		    a reader nothing to skip to, and the labels were the same small-bold
		    kicker shape the home page dropped.

		    ⚠ THE LEAD IS GONE (2026-08-10, with the about-page scale — see the
		    header note). The register change the dropped headings used to mark
		    — context, then what I did — rode the TYPE from 08-01 on: the problem
		    paragraph was a step up in size (23.75px) and set in --ink-soft, so
		    the work paragraph read as the body it stepped down into. The about
		    page sets every paragraph identically, so matching it spends that
		    device: both are now 16.625px in plain --ink and nothing but the
		    paragraph break separates them. That is a real loss and it was the
		    price of the ask, not an oversight — if the two paragraphs ever need
		    telling apart again, the headings are the honest fix, because the
		    type has no step left to make.

		    Nothing here is capped by these numbers, but the MEASURE now is: the
		    section is still max-w-[var(--measure)] (35rem / 665px), which held
		    ~11 words a line at 21.375px and holds ~80 characters at 16.625 —
		    past the usual 45–75. The about page's own column is 511.5px and ~62
		    characters. Narrowing this is the obvious follow-up and was left
		    alone deliberately: it is a layout change, and the ask was sizes and
		    colours. The page itself still spans the nav's full 960px, which the
		    images below fill. */}
		<section class="reveal mt-8 max-w-[var(--measure)]" style="--i: 1">
			{/* ── 2026-08-11: no weight override, so these inherit --weight-body ──
			    Hiro's ask, "make the body font weight lighter". Both paragraphs
			    carried [font-weight:var(--weight-ui)] (w400 dark / w500 light);
			    dropping it takes them to w300/w400. This is the change the
			    previous note here named as the exact-match move — the paragraphs
			    now render at the about page's size, colour AND weight, and this
			    page has no type of its own left except the h1's size.

			    ⚠ WHAT THAT OVERRIDE WAS HOLDING, because it was not decoration:
			    it was the 2026-08-04 legibility audit's finding, the only weight
			    bump that audit required, and the one thing the 08-10 about-page
			    pass deliberately left standing on the argument that weight is
			    neither a size nor a colour and this is the site's longest-form
			    reading. That argument has now been overruled by the ask, which is
			    Hiro's call to make; it is recorded here so the bump is not
			    re-applied as a "fix" by someone who finds the audit and not this.

			    The audit's own numbers are STALE and are not restated: they were
			    measured at 23.75px/--ink-soft and 21.375px/--ink, and both
			    paragraphs are 16.625px/--ink now. What survives the restatement is
			    the direction — smaller type needs MORE weight for the same APCA
			    floor, and this went the other way at 16.625px, so assume the
			    margin is thinner than anything the audit recorded. Re-measure
			    before quoting a figure.

			    It is not an unprecedented posture: the about page's prose is
			    16.625px at w300 in this same ink, and the audit already carries
			    that as a standing contrast item weight cannot fix. This page has
			    joined it rather than invented a worse one. If the floor ever has
			    to be met for real, --ink-heading on the prose buys more than any
			    weight tier does at this size, and colour is the lever the 08-10
			    pass already used to retire the lead's shortfall. */}
			<p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">{project.problem}</p>
			<p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed">{project.work}</p>

			{/* ── The link out (2026-08-16, Hiro: add links to migaki and ejs.co on
			    the respective project pages) ──
			    ⚠ It renders off `linkLabel`, NOT off `link`. Four of the five
			    projects carry a URL and only two carry a label, so a `project.link
			    && …` guard here would publish links on three pages nobody asked
			    about — see the field's note in projects.ts for the whole argument.
			    The `link &&` half of the condition below is a type guard, not a
			    second switch; a label without a URL is a data error, and this
			    renders nothing rather than an <a> with no href.

			    ── WHY .btn-quiet, and why this is not the Visit button returning ──
			    That style has been ORPHANED since 08-10, when the back-to-projects
			    link (its only consumer) came off this page, and its note in
			    global.css left it standing on the explicit grounds that "the next
			    borderless text link on the site wants exactly this". This is that
			    link. It arrives with the whole family already decided — 0.875rem,
			    --weight-ui, the site's one underline (instant in, --dur-pop-out
			    out), a focus ring — so there is no new treatment on the page.

			    ── IT IS CYAN (2026-08-16, Hiro), which is the one thing the
			    family does NOT decide for it ──
			    Hence the --out modifier: --ink-link at rest instead of the
			    family's --ink-soft, and no colour change on hover, because an
			    accent has nowhere to darken TO — the family's hover ink is a grey.
			    Read .btn-quiet--out in global.css for the argument, and for why
			    its hover rule looks like a no-op and isn't. What the colour buys
			    on THIS page is worth stating: the link sits a few lines above a
			    figcaption set at the same size, and --ink-soft separated the two
			    by half a step of grey. Cyan separates them by kind, which is what
			    they actually differ by.

			    The 07-29 removal that took the tags also took a "Visit" button,
			    and the thing that made it worth removing was that it was a BOXED
			    .btn: the only piece of enclosed chrome on an otherwise borderless
			    layout. That is the reason .btn-quiet exists at all. Borderless is
			    the fix, so this is not a reversal of that call.

			    THE ARROW IS THE ↗, and the family's note names this exact glyph as
			    the one meaning it carries: this leaves the site. It is also the
			    site's existing signal for target="_blank" (Nav.astro says so at the
			    contact slider), so the mark and the behaviour agree. No tilt on it
			    — a rotation would encode a second meaning and there isn't one; the
			    only arrow on the site that turns is the projects toggle, where the
			    turn IS open-vs-shut. And no hover nudge: nothing on this control
			    moves for a cursor (2026-08-16, and the family lost its nudge the
			    same day).

			    Drawn in the family's idiom — 24 box, line + polyline, stroke 2,
			    round caps — spanning 5→19 rather than a stock icon's 7→17. That is
			    optical, not cosmetic: at width 14 the shaft has to cover roughly
			    the same fraction of its box as the toggle's ↓ (which runs 4→19) or
			    a diagonal reads a size smaller sitting next to it. The head's legs
			    are 10 units, which is the right arrow's 9.9 to the pixel.

			    ── PLACEMENT: end of the prose, not the header and not the foot ──
			    Inside this section, so it rides the prose's own --i: 1 beat and
			    enters as part of the block it belongs to rather than as a fifth
			    thing. It is also inside --measure, so it starts on the same left
			    edge as every line above it.
			    NOT in the header: that is a settled two-line composition (year,
			    title) and a link is a third register in it.
			    NOT below the screenshots: the only gap down there is the 114px
			    before "More projects", which was measured as an empty span and
			    reads as one — a control parked in it would both break that and
			    compete with the strip for the same click.

			    mt-8 (38px) is chosen against its neighbours, which is the only way
			    this page sets a gap: 28.5px between the two paragraphs, 47.5px from
			    here to the screenshots. Sitting between the two makes the link
			    further from the prose than the prose is from itself, and closer to
			    it than the images are — so it reads as the end of the reading, not
			    as a section of its own. The wrapper is a block <div> because
			    .btn-quiet is inline-flex; margin on an atomic inline works but
			    lands in a line box, and the div keeps the gap the plain 38px this
			    note claims. */}
			{project.linkLabel && project.link && (
				<div class="mt-8">
					<a href={project.link} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-quiet btn-quiet--out">
						<span class="btn-quiet__label">{project.linkLabel}</span>
						<svg
							class="btn-quiet__arrow"
							viewBox="0 0 24 24"
							fill="none"
							stroke="currentColor"
							stroke-width="2"
							stroke-linecap="round"
							stroke-linejoin="round"
							aria-hidden="true"
							width="14"
							height="14"
						>
							<line x1="5" y1="19" x2="19" y2="5"></line>
							<polyline points="9 5 19 5 19 15"></polyline>
						</svg>
					</a>
				</div>
			)}
		</section>

		<section class="reveal mt-10 space-y-5" style="--i: 2">
			{/* Placeholder frames for a project whose screenshots don't exist yet.
			    Same border, radius and bed as the real images below, but at the
			    cards' 16/9 — a real screenshot sets its own height from the file,
			    and an empty div has none to take, so the ratio has to be stated.
			    They hold the page's shape (and the reveal's third beat) so
			    dropping the images in later is a data change, nothing more. */}
			{project.images.length === 0 &&
				Array.from({ length: project.placeholders ?? 0 }).map(() => (
					<div class="aspect-[16/9] w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface-sunken)] bg-clip-padding" />
				))}
			{/* ── The screenshots open (2026-08-14, Hiro: hovering an image should
			    give the zoom cursor, clicking should open it full screen) ──
			    Each shot is wrapped in a real <button>, not a click handler on the
			    <img>. The wrapper is what makes the gesture reachable by keyboard
			    and announced as a control; an img with a listener is neither, and
			    this page's screenshots are the only thing on it worth enlarging.
			    The button is a bare block box (see .shot in global.css) — the
			    border, radius and bed still belong to the <img>, so the rendered
			    page is pixel-identical to what it was before the wrapper existed.

			    The aria-label names the POSITION ("image 2 of 5") rather than
			    repeating the alt text underneath it, which the button already
			    exposes; a screen reader reaching this hears the alt, then what the
			    control does with it.

			    ⚠ THE BUTTON IS ONLY A BUTTON ON A POINTER DEVICE (2026-08-14,
			    Hiro: mobile shouldn't have any zooming in or out, since you can
			    zoom with your fingers on the page directly). The markup is static
			    and identical everywhere, so the script downstairs is what decides:
			    on a coarse pointer it takes the tab stop and the button role back
			    off these and never opens anything. Read that block before treating
			    this element as unconditionally interactive — it is markup for the
			    case where a cursor exists.

			    ── The <figure> wrapper and the caption (merged in from the caption
			    branch, 2026-08-14) ──
			    figure > figcaption + button > img, and that NESTING IS THE WHOLE
			    RESOLUTION. The caption sits outside the button on purpose: inside,
			    it would join the button's hit area — so clicking a label would open
			    a lightbox — and it would be swallowed by the control's accessible
			    name. Outside, it stays a caption of the figure and the button stays
			    a control wrapping exactly one image. Don't collapse these two
			    wrappers into one; they answer different questions.

			    CAPTION FIRST, above the image. Spec-legal — figcaption may be the
			    figure's first or last child — and it earns the position: a reader
			    cannot un-see a picture, so a label underneath arrives after the
			    impression it was meant to frame, which for a before/after is
			    exactly backwards.

			    The figure is now the section's space-y-5 child, which is what gives
			    the caption a gap of its own to be tighter than — the one inside the
			    figure has to stay well under that 5. */}
			{
				figures.map(({ src, w, h, caption }, i) => (
					<figure>
						{caption && <figcaption class={captionClass}>{caption}</figcaption>}
						{/* ── SHIPS INERT, PROMOTED BY SCRIPT (2026-08-18) ──
						    disabled + role="presentation" + NO aria-label is the
						    resting state, and all three are load-bearing. ARIA's
						    presentational-roles conflict resolution drops the role
						    if the element is FOCUSABLE or carries a global ARIA
						    attribute, and a <button> is inherently focusable — so
						    role="presentation" alone is silently ignored (verified
						    against Chrome's AX tree: it stayed role=button, named).
						    `disabled` is what makes it genuinely unfocusable and
						    lets the role finally apply; the label lives in data-*
						    until the script has a device that can use it.
						    The <img> keeps its alt throughout — the role applies to
						    the wrapper alone, not its subtree.

						    Inert is also the RIGHT DEFAULT for the pre-JS window:
						    the module is deferred, so shipping a live button means
						    a touch screen reader announces one control per figure
						    that does nothing until it lands. Absent-then-appearing beats
						    present-and-lying. See syncPointerState below. */}
						<button
							type="button"
							class="shot"
							data-shot
							data-shot-label={`Enlarge image ${i + 1} of ${figures.length}`}
							role="presentation"
							disabled
						>
							{/* ── width/height are NOT the render size (2026-08-17) ──
							    They are the file's own pixels, and the only thing the
							    browser takes from them here is the ratio: preflight's
							    height:auto still decides the height and w-full still
							    decides the width, so these change nothing you can see
							    on a loaded page. What they change is the page BEFORE
							    it loads — with no ratio, height:auto is zero, and
							    this section laid out as a stack of slivers that
							    snapped to ~2,300px as the files arrived, walking the
							    link-out, More projects and the footer down the page.

							    They are also what makes the lazy below real: while
							    every box was 0px tall they all sat at the top of the
							    document, inside the viewport, so nothing was ever
							    deferred and the browser pulled all four at once.

							    Don't drop either attribute to "let CSS handle it" —
							    CSS is handling it, and this is what it needs. */}
							<img
								src={src}
								width={w}
								height={h}
								alt={caption ? `${project.title} — ${caption}` : `${project.title} — image ${i + 1}`}
								loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
								class="w-full rounded-[var(--radius)] border border-[var(--line)] bg-[var(--surface)]"
							/>
						</button>
					</figure>
				))
			}
		</section>

		{/* ── More projects (2026-08-10, Hiro) ──
		    A strip of the other work at the foot of the case study. It is the
		    same ProjectGrid the home page uses, with the same travelling hover
		    panel — see that component for why it became one rather than being
		    copied. A visitor who reaches the bottom of a project page has
		    finished the only thing this page had to say; the strip is what it
		    hands off to, and it is also the page's way back into the work now
		    that the back-to-projects button is gone (removed earlier today —
		    see the footer note below for what that removal took with it).

		    THE HEADING IS A REAL h2, at the about page's exact treatment
		    (text-xl / --weight-emph / tracking-tight / --ink-heading). This page
		    took that page's scale and palette this morning, so there is nothing
		    to decide here — the value already exists. Not the small-bold
		    uppercase kicker: this codebase has dropped that shape twice (the
		    Background/Work labels on this very page, and the about page's
		    interests heading), and it is not coming back through a side door.

		    ── NO DIVIDER (2026-08-10, Hiro: remove the line between the images
		    and this strip) ── The break is carried by WHITESPACE ALONE now, and
		    it is the only section break on the site drawn that way: the nav's
		    edge, the home page's projects line and the about card's two section
		    rules are all still ruled. So if a later pass is "making the dividers
		    consistent", this one is the deliberate exception, not the omission.

		    THE GAP DID NOT CHANGE WITH IT — and then, a day later, it had to.
		    The rule's removal kept the distance to the pixel: mt-20 (95px) + a
		    1px border + pt-12 (57px) = 153px became a single mt-32 (152px), so
		    the space the line had sat in stayed exactly where it was, which also
		    kept the removal cheap to reverse.

		    ── mt-24, 114px (2026-08-11, Hiro: is there too much space above More
		    projects) ── Yes, and preserving the number is what caused it. 152px
		    was calibrated for a gap with a LINE IN THE MIDDLE OF IT: the eye
		    never met 152px of continuous emptiness, it met 95px, a rule, then
		    57px. Take the rule out and the same distance becomes one unbroken
		    void, and unbroken whitespace reads far larger than the same distance
		    interrupted. The gap did not grow; it emptied. Holding the number
		    preserved the measurement and lost the effect.

		    MEASURED on this page, which is what settled it: every other gap here
		    is 47.5px (prose → screenshots) or 38px (this h2 → its cards), and
		    the page ENDS on 123.5px (see the main's sm:pb-16). At mt-32 the
		    break in the middle of the page was larger than the one that closes
		    it — the longest pause landed before the strip rather than after it,
		    which reads as the strip being detached from the case study instead
		    of following it. Detached is precisely what the whitespace was meant
		    to avoid when the rule went. 114px puts it back under the page's
		    closing gap and still leaves it 2.4× the next-largest gap here, which
		    is all the strip needs to read as its own movement.

		    ⚠ THE REVERSAL IS NO LONGER FREE, and the note this replaces said it
		    was: 20/12 + a .card-rule restored the old arrangement precisely only
		    while this was mt-32. Putting the rule back now means restoring the
		    gap as well, or accepting a rule in a 114px space it was never
		    measured for.

		    Still the largest bare gap on the site, and still load bearing:
		    whitespace is doing a job three other places on the site give a line.
		    The next step down is mt-20 (95px), which is where it would start
		    competing with the 47.5px gaps above it rather than out-ranking them.

		    The lopsidedness went with the rule, and had to. 95-above/57-below
		    copied the home page's arrangement at #projects, on the argument that
		    a rule introduces the section under it and so belongs nearer to it.
		    With no rule there is nothing for the asymmetry to describe — it was
		    never about the two blocks, it was about where the LINE sat between
		    them — so one margin is the honest shape.

		    What the rule WAS, kept because the choice was not obvious and would
		    otherwise be re-litigated: a .card-rule, deliberately not
		    #projects::before. Same paint either way — both are drawn off the
		    shared --rule gradient — but #projects::before is keyed to that id
		    and carries the load finale's `rule-grow` animation (global.css),
		    tuned to the INDEX's two-block cascade. Borrowing the id would have
		    pulled that animation onto a rule several thousand pixels below the
		    fold, where it would play unseen and out of step with this page's
		    four-block cascade, and it would have put a second #projects on the
		    site. .card-rule is back to its original two consumers (the about
		    card's section dividers), so it is not orphaned by this.

		    The optical correction those about-card rules need never applied
		    here anyway, and applies even less now: the thing above this gap is
		    an <img>, which has no half-leading, so its bottom edge is its ink
		    and the margin paints exactly what it says.

		    mt-8 (38px) from the h2 down to the grid, not the about page's mt-4.
		    That gap is smaller than it looks: a card's p-3/-m-3 pair overflows
		    its grid track by 14.25px on every side, so the HOVER PANEL's top
		    edge sits 14.25px above the grid element's own top edge. At mt-4 a
		    hovered first-row card would light up a tint within ~5px of the
		    heading's descenders. 38 leaves 23.75px of clearance, which is the
		    same figure as the panel's own overhang.

		    The guard is for a one-project site, not for today: with a single
		    project left, `others` is empty and the whole strip goes rather than
		    rendering a heading over nothing. It reads `> 1` rather than `> 0`
		    deliberately — one lone card sitting at a third of the grid's width
		    under a plural heading is worse than no strip at all. Five projects
		    today and `others` is capped at three, so this is always one full
		    row of three — see the `others` note for WHICH three, and for why
		    that cap is a rotation rather than a truncation.

		    ── columns={3} (2026-08-11, Hiro: make these smaller so they all fit
		    in one row and fit the width of the page) ── The default is 2, which
		    is the home page's grid; at three cards that left one alone on row
		    two. The cards get NARROWER rather than the strip getting a type
		    scale of its own: ProjectCard is untouched, so the year, the title
		    and the 16/9 frame are the same components the home page draws, in a
		    285.17px track instead of a 442px one. That is the whole of
		    "smaller", and ProjectGrid.astro records where dividing further stops
		    working — and note the ⚠ there about px figures being at a 19px root,
		    because these two numbers are the ones most likely to be re-derived
		    wrongly from the class names.

		    The grid still spans the page's full 912.5px — the width never
		    changed, only how many ways it is cut. Note the strip is the ONLY
		    thing on this page at that width: the prose above is capped at
		    --measure (665px) and the screenshots fill 912.5px. So the strip and
		    the images share an edge and the cards' outer edges line up with
		    them, which is what keeps a three-up row from reading as a different
		    page element.

		    ── The card images share a bottom edge (2026-08-11, Hiro: the image
		    should hug the bottom of the card) ── That is ProjectCard's mt-auto,
		    not anything this page sets, and this strip is the only place on the
		    site where it changes a pixel — see the note there. It exists because
		    the three-up row is the first row on the site to mix one-line and
		    two-line titles. */}
		{others.length > 1 && (
			<section class="reveal mt-24" style="--i: 3">
				<h2 class="text-xl [font-weight:var(--weight-emph)] tracking-tight text-[var(--ink-heading)]">
					More projects
				</h2>
				{/* No .reveal on the grid — this section is the reveal, and a .reveal
				    inside a .reveal animates opacity against a parent already doing
				    the same, so the block would arrive twice. The heading and the
				    cards enter together as one object, which is the rule the about
				    card and the home page's hero both follow. --i 3, one beat behind
				    the screenshots at 2. */}
				<ProjectGrid projects={others} columns={3} class="mt-8" />
			</section>
		)}
	</main>

	{/* ── The copyright footer (2026-08-10, Hiro: add a footer) ──
	    Lifted verbatim from the home page, exactly as the about page's was
	    (08-05) and for the same reason: same element, same max-w-[960px]/px-5
	    so the line sits on the SAME left edge as the prose, the h2 above it and
	    the leftmost strip card's year, same py-10, same text-xs on --ink-soft.
	    A copyright line is the lowest-priority text on the site and the home
	    page settled what that looks like; a third treatment would be a third
	    answer to a solved question. All three routes now end the same way.

	    OUTSIDE <main>, matching both. It isn't page content and the reveal
	    cascade doesn't touch it — no .reveal, no --i, so it is simply there
	    when the page is.

	    NOTE this replaces a footer that was a BACK LINK rather than a
	    copyright, and about.astro's footer note used to cite that difference as
	    the reason it took the home page's version instead. That distinction is
	    gone: there is one footer on the site now, in three places. The note
	    over there has been corrected.

	    ── What the old footer was, and what removing it took with it ──
	    It held exactly one thing: a "Back to projects" link whose slug sent you
	    to the CARD you came from, at the scroll position you left it. It was the
	    last navigation on the page, several thousand pixels of screenshots below
	    where you started. That job is now the strip above — different in kind,
	    since the strip sends you ONWARD to another project rather than back to
	    the grid — plus the browser's Back button and the wordmark.

	    ⚠ IT WAS ALSO THE ENTRY POINT TO THE WHOLE PROJECTS-LANDING PIPELINE:
	    that anchor carried `data-projects-link`, and it was the last element
	    on the site that did — the nav's own Projects link went on 08-01. With
	    nothing left to bind to, everything downstream was unreachable rather
	    than merely unused (Nav.astro's click handler, `goToProjects()`, the
	    `nav-projects` intent and its `.projects-landing` curtain,
	    `landOnProjects()`, the `nav-return` scroll recorder, and the
	    reveal-hold-land / reveal-skip classes), so it was all removed on
	    2026-08-18 — see that commit, not this file, to revive it. One thing
	    the old machinery never handled, if it ever is revived: the strip
	    above means a card click can now happen ON A PROJECT PAGE, so a scroll
	    recorder has to be scoped to the home page (or keyed by origin as well
	    as slug) — otherwise it records this page's scrollY under the target
	    project's slug, a Y measured on a page you would never restore to. */}
	<footer class="mx-auto max-w-[960px] px-5 py-10 text-xs text-[var(--ink-soft)]">
		<span>© 2026 Hiro Eernisse</span>
	</footer>

	{/* ── The lightbox (2026-08-14, Hiro) ──
	    ONE dialog for the whole page, re-pointed at whichever shot was clicked,
	    rather than one per image. There is only ever one open, the markup for a
	    fourth screenshot would be identical to the first three, and a single
	    element means a single place for the dismissal handler to live.

	    ── IT CLOSES WHEREVER YOU CLICK, AND THERE IS NO CLOSE BUTTON
	    (2026-08-14, Hiro, revising the same day's first version) ──
	    The zoom cursor is the entire control surface: zoom-in over a shot on the
	    page, zoom-out over every pixel of the open modal, image included. The
	    gesture reverses itself, so the thing that opened it closes it.

	    It shipped with an X chip fixed in the top-right corner for a few hours.
	    What the chip was actually for was TOUCH — a cursor cannot be an
	    affordance where there is no cursor — and the answer to that turned out
	    to be that touch has no business here at all: a phone pinch-zooms the
	    page itself, far better than any modal would. So the feature is now
	    gated to pointer devices (see the script), and once mobile is out of
	    scope the chip has nobody left to serve. The cost, accepted knowingly:
	    clicking a screenshot to point at something in it closes it.

	    A NATIVE <dialog> opened with showModal(), which is doing more work here
	    than it looks. Four behaviours come free and are NOT reimplemented below,
	    so read this before "adding" any of them:
	      · Esc closes it (the platform's close request);
	      · it renders in the TOP LAYER, above everything, so it needs no z-index
	        and cannot be trapped by the nav's stacking context or the page's
	        transformed .reveal blocks — either of which would defeat a plain
	        fixed-position div;
	      · the rest of the page goes inert, so a tab press can't land on a nav
	        link behind the image, and focus returns to the shot you opened when
	        it closes;
	      · ::backdrop is a real element to paint, so the scrim isn't a div.

	    closedby="any" asks the browser for LIGHT DISMISS — a click on the
	    backdrop closes it with no script at all. With the whole modal now a
	    dismiss target the script covers that case anyway, so this is belt and
	    braces rather than the mechanism; it stays because it means the scrim
	    keeps working if the module never loads. It is still why the dialog
	    shrink-wraps the image (see .lightbox in global.css) instead of filling
	    the viewport: "outside" has to be outside the dialog's own box for the
	    browser to count it.

	    THE DIALOG IS THE ONLY FOCUSABLE THING IN HERE now that the close button
	    is gone — showModal() focuses the dialog element itself, the aria-label
	    below is what a screen reader announces on arrival, and Esc is the way
	    out without a pointer. */}
	<dialog id="lightbox" class="lightbox" closedby="any" aria-label="Enlarged screenshot">
		{/* src and alt are both set by the script — empty here so the page never
		    fetches a second copy of a screenshot nobody has asked to enlarge. */}
		<img class="lightbox__img" alt="" />
	</dialog>
</Layout>

<script>
	const dialog = document.getElementById('lightbox') as HTMLDialogElement | null;
	const full = dialog?.querySelector<HTMLImageElement>('.lightbox__img');

	if (dialog && full) {
		// ── The whole feature is gated on there being a POINTER ──
		// (2026-08-14, Hiro: mobile shouldn't have any zooming in or out, since
		// you can zoom with your fingers on the page directly.) A phone already
		// has a better version of this gesture than any modal could offer — it
		// pinch-zooms the real page, in place, at whatever magnification the
		// reader wants, with no state to get out of. Opening a fixed-size copy
		// of the same picture on top of it is strictly worse.
		//
		// `(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)` is the pair, not either alone:
		// hover alone lets in a stylus, and pointer alone lets in a TV remote.
		// Both together mean a mouse or a trackpad, which is exactly the device
		// class where a cursor can carry the affordance.
		//
		// A LIVE QUERY, read inside each handler rather than branched on once at
		// startup: an iPad that gets a trackpad attached mid-session flips this
		// from false to true, and a laptop user who picks the screen up flips it
		// back. Reading it per event means the feature simply follows the device
		// rather than being decided by whatever was true when the page loaded.
		const pointer = matchMedia('(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)');

		const shots = document.querySelectorAll<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-shot]');

		// The markup ships a <button> unconditionally (it has to — the page is
		// static), but it ships it INERT: disabled, role="presentation", no
		// label. So this promotes on a fine pointer rather than demoting on a
		// coarse one, and the coarse state is simply the one the HTML already
		// arrived in — nothing to wait for, nothing announced in the meantime.
		// The <img> keeps its alt on both sides; role presentation applies to
		// the wrapper alone, not to its contents.
		//
		// ⚠ ALL FOUR ATTRIBUTES MOVE TOGETHER, and the pair that looks
		// redundant is not (2026-08-18). This previously toggled tabIndex and
		// role only, which did NOTHING: ARIA's presentational-roles conflict
		// resolution ignores role="presentation" on an element that is
		// focusable or carries a global ARIA attribute, and a <button> with
		// tabindex="-1" is still focusable and still had its aria-label. Chrome
		// went on exposing it as role=button name="Enlarge image N of M" on
		// every phone, which is the exact thing the rule above exists to
		// prevent. `disabled` is the part that makes it unfocusable so the role
		// can apply, and the label has to be OFF the element for the same
		// reason — hence data-shot-label. Drop any one of them and the wrapper
		// silently becomes an announced button again.
		//
		// Checked both directions against Chrome's AX tree, since a trackpad
		// arriving mid-session has to flip this live: promoted gives
		// role=button with the label, demoted removes the node entirely.
		const syncPointerState = () => {
			shots.forEach((shot) => {
				if (pointer.matches) {
					shot.disabled = false;
					shot.removeAttribute('role');
					shot.tabIndex = 0;
					shot.setAttribute('aria-label', shot.dataset.shotLabel ?? '');
				} else {
					shot.disabled = true;
					shot.setAttribute('role', 'presentation');
					shot.tabIndex = -1;
					shot.removeAttribute('aria-label');
				}
			});
		};

		syncPointerState();
		pointer.addEventListener('change', syncPointerState);

		shots.forEach((shot) => {
			shot.addEventListener('click', () => {
				if (!pointer.matches) return;
				const img = shot.querySelector('img');
				if (!img) return;
				// currentSrc, not src: it is the file the browser actually chose and
				// already has in cache, so the enlarged view paints instantly instead
				// of starting a second request. They are the same string today (no
				// srcset on these), which is exactly why this is worth stating — the
				// day a srcset appears, this keeps working.
				full.src = img.currentSrc || img.src;
				full.alt = img.alt;
				dialog.showModal();
			});
		});

		// A click ANYWHERE in the modal closes it — scrim or picture, no target
		// check, which is the whole of the dismissal now. The zoom-out cursor
		// runs over the same area (see .lightbox in global.css), so the pointer
		// is never over a pixel that promises something this doesn't do.
		//
		// ⚠ THIS USED TO BE FOUR TIMES LONGER, and the deleted version was not
		// wrong — it is worth knowing what it was solving before writing it
		// again. It tracked pointerdown and pointerup separately and required
		// both ends of the press to land on the scrim, because a `click` reports
		// the nearest common ancestor of press and release: a drag starting on
		// the image and ending on the scrim was indistinguishable from a scrim
		// click, and closed the picture you were dragging on. That mattered only
		// while the image was NOT a dismiss target. Now that everything is one,
		// there are no two cases left to tell apart, and the guard would be
		// machinery for a distinction the design no longer draws.
		dialog.addEventListener('click', () => dialog.close());
	}
</script>
